    %p.eyebrow Cragmont Climbing Club
    %h1 History
    = render "navigation"
    .club-copy.content-page-markdown
      %p.club-lead From Berkeley practice rocks to Yosemite granite, the club's name carries nearly a century of climbing history.
      .club-timeline
        %section
          %p.club-year 1931–32
          %div
            %h2 A club takes shape
            %p Jules Eichorn, Dick Leonard, Bestor Robinson, and other Bay Area climbers practiced rope techniques on Berkeley rocks. They formed the original Cragmont Climbing Club in March 1932, naming it for one of their local climbing areas. Later that year, the group joined the Sierra Club's new Rock Climbing Section.
        %section
          %p.club-year 1932–33
          %div
            %h2 Learning to climb safely
            %p The group trained relentlessly on belaying and rappelling before taking ropes to Yosemite. Their experiments helped develop the hip belay and more dynamic ways to catch a fall—important steps in American climbing technique.
        %section
          %p.club-year 1933
          %div
            %h2 First roped climbs in Yosemite
            %p On Labor Day weekend, club climbers traveled to Yosemite Valley. Leonard, Eichorn, Robinson, and Hervey Voge climbed partway up Washington Column to the place later called Lunch Ledge. They turned back rather than rush into harder terrain above.
        %section
          %p.club-year 1934
          %div
            %h2 The Cathedral Spires
            %p Leonard, Eichorn, and Robinson reached the summit of Higher Cathedral Spire in April and Lower Cathedral Spire in August. Their attempts combined careful preparation, a willingness to retreat, and the early use of aid on Yosemite granite.
        %section
          %p.club-year 1988
          %div
            %h2 The club continues
            %p A new Cragmont Climbing Club formed to continue the community and outings of the Sierra Club Rock Climbing Section after organized climbing ended there. The Berkeley name and its climbing tradition lived on.
      %p.club-source The timeline above is a brief adaptation. The longer story continues below.
      %article#longer-history.club-history-article
        %header
          %p.eyebrow The longer story
          %h2 From Cragmont Rock to Yosemite
          %p.club-lead The club began on small Berkeley rocks, but its early climbers were already asking how to move safely on much bigger walls.
          %p.club-history-byline A modern retelling of the club's early years
        %section
          %h3 A name from the local rocks
          %p In the early 1930s, Jules Eichorn, Dick Leonard, Bestor Robinson, and friends practiced climbing and ropework on the modest outcrops above Berkeley. Their group became the Cragmont Climbing Club in March 1932, taking its name from one of those rocks. By that November it had joined the Sierra Club's new Bay Area Rock Climbing Section. The name changed, but the small practice area remained the starting point for a much larger story.
          %p These were not full-time adventurers. Many were students or young professionals with limited time and money during the Depression. Local sessions mattered because they could not simply spend every weekend in the Sierra. The group built a habit of learning together before taking on unfamiliar terrain.
        %section
          %h3 Training for a different scale
          %p The climbers worked on belaying, rappelling, and catching falls until rope handling felt dependable. They helped develop the hip belay and more dynamic ways of stopping a fall, techniques that influenced American climbing well beyond their own outings. Their preparation was not only about equipment. It was also a decision to treat Yosemite's steep granite as a place that demanded care, not bravado.
          %p On Labor Day weekend in 1933, a group traveled to Yosemite Valley. Leonard, Eichorn, Robinson, and Hervey Voge tried Washington Column. They reached the ledge later called Lunch Ledge, high above the Valley floor, but did not force a route to the summit. The hour and the terrain argued for a retreat. Their partial climb showed what the group could do with ropes while leaving the harder ground for another day.
        %section
          %h3 Two Cathedral Spires
          %p The Higher Cathedral Spire became the next great objective. The three Berkeley partners studied the formation, returned after unsuccessful attempts, and brought pitons and carabiners to help them cross short, steep sections. On April 15, 1934, Leonard, Eichorn, and Robinson reached its summit. National Park Service records preserve some of the hardware from that ascent.
          %p The same team reached the Lower Cathedral Spire on August 25, 1934. Its difficulties called for more improvisation, including a passage where the rock was altered to create footholds. That choice belongs to its time; it also reminds us that climbing ethics evolve. Their enduring example is not a claim that every decision was perfect, but a combination of practice, ingenuity, boldness, and willingness to turn back.
        %section
          %h3 The community continues
          %p The present Cragmont Climbing Club formed in 1988 to continue the outings and friendships of the Sierra Club Rock Climbing Section. Today's trips are different from those early Yosemite experiments, but the name still connects local partners, shared responsibility, and days spent outside together.
